    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-11-429-2018",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Grassland management affects the carbon fluxes of one-third of the global land area and is thus an important factor for the global carbon budget. Nonetheless, this aspect has been largely neglected or underrepresented in global carbon cycle models. We investigate four harvesting schemes for the managed grassland implementation of the dynamic global vegetation model (DGVM) Lund\u2013Potsdam\u2013Jena managed Land (LPJmL) that facilitate a better representation of actual management systems globally. We describe the model implementation and analyze simulation results with respect to harvest, net primary productivity and soil carbon content and by evaluating them against reported grass yields in Europe. We demonstrate the importance of accounting for differences in grassland management by assessing potential livestock grazing densities as well as the impacts of grazing, grazing intensities and mowing systems on soil carbon stocks. Grazing leads to soil carbon losses in polar or arid regions even at moderate livestock densities (&lt; 0.4 livestock units per hectare \u2013 LSU ha\u22121) but not in temperate regions even at much higher densities (0.4 to 1.2 LSU ha\u22121). Applying LPJmL with the new grassland management options enables assessments of the global grassland production and its impact on the terrestrial biogeochemical cycles but requires a global data set on current grassland management.",
    "field": "Modeling of managed grasslands",
    "problem": "Global carbon cycle models have largely neglected or underrepresented diverse grassland management practices, a significant shortcoming as these systems affect the carbon fluxes of one-third of the global land area and are thus an important factor for the global carbon budget.",
    "method": "The paper extends the LPJmL dynamic global vegetation model by implementing four explicit harvesting schemes\u2014representing different mowing and grazing systems\u2014to better simulate the vegetation and carbon dynamics of managed grasslands and assess their impact on the global carbon budget.",
    "task": "The task is to implement and evaluate four new grassland management schemes (mowing and grazing) in the global vegetation model LPJmL 3.6 to better represent their impact on vegetation and carbon dynamics.",
